{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Instead of fragrance.</b> He will appoint a substitute and strengthen it." "Now <b>מק</b> is close to a transposition, and it belongs to the doubled-consonant verbs, and from its root is תמקנה בחוריהן (Zechariah 14:12)." "<b>A rope.</b> A noun, and the nun is a root letter, and it is close in derivation to ונקף סבכי היער (Isaiah 10:34), and its meaning is like cutting down." "<b>Ma'aseh miqsha.</b> Shaving the hair evenly, like ולא עשה רגליו (1 Samuel 19:25)." "<b>A fine tunic.</b> A thin embroidered garment placed over all the garments." "<b>Instead of beauty.</b> The Gaon [Saadia] said that the opposite, which is 'instead of,' is more beautiful to them, and others said that the word כי is from the root כויה (Exodus 21:24), and like it is אף ברי (Job 37:11), which is from the root רווי, but this is not correct in grammar, for the vav of כויה is a root letter, and in my opinion it is thus: instead of beauty — the מק and the נקפה."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "Reading מהפך as 'transposition' (metathesis), referring to the transposed/irregular form of the root.", 2 "The lemma נקפה (from Isa 3:24, meaning a cord/rope) is derived from the root נ-ק-ף."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:13.150685528Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}